/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:1, name:"A People Sick With Sin", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 1:18",verse:"Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool."}, facts:["Did you know the book of Isaiah opens by naming four kings of Judah, Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, which places Isaiah's ministry across roughly sixty years of the eighth century BC?","Did you know this chapter reads like a courtroom trial, with heaven and earth called as witnesses while the LORD brings His charge against His own people?","Did you know the scarlet to snow promise in verse 18 is one of the most quoted lines in all of Isaiah?"], questions:[
{q:"Isaiah saw his vision in the days of four kings of Judah. Which of these was one of them?", options:["Hezekiah","David","Solomon","Josiah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:1",verse:"The vision of Isaiah the son of Amoz, which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem in the days of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, kings of Judah.", insight:"Isaiah ministered across four reigns, a long public witness through good kings and bad ones alike, which teaches that faithfulness is measured by steady obedience, not by who sits on the throne."},
{q:"Whom did the LORD call as witnesses when He spoke against rebellious Israel?", options:["The heavens and the earth","The kings of the east","The prophets of Baal","The ox and the ass"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:2",verse:"Hear, O heavens, and give ear, O earth: for the LORD hath spoken, I have nourished and brought up children, and they have rebelled against me.", insight:"Calling heaven and earth to witness shows this is a covenant case, not a passing complaint. God takes His relationship with His people seriously enough to make His charge in open court."},
{q:"Which two animals does Isaiah say know their master better than Israel knew God?", options:["The ox and the ass","The sheep and the goat","The horse and the mule","The dove and the raven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:3",verse:"The ox knoweth his owner, and the ass his master’s crib: but Israel doth not know, my people doth not consider.", insight:"The ox knows his owner and the ass his master's crib, yet God's people did not consider. Knowing facts about God is not the same as knowing Him, and animals that trust their keeper put to shame the people who would not."},
{q:"The sinful nation had forsaken the LORD and provoked what name of God to anger?", options:["The Holy One of Israel","The God of the east","The King of the nations","The Father of lights"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:4",verse:"Ah sinful nation, a people laden with iniquity, a seed of evildoers, children that are corrupters: they have forsaken the LORD, they have provoked the Holy One of Israel unto anger, they are gone away backward.", insight:"Isaiah's favorite name for God stresses His holiness, the very thing sin assaults. To provoke the Holy One is to set yourself against His nature, which is why sin is never a small matter."},
{q:"How far had the nation's sickness spread, according to Isaiah's body picture?", options:["The whole head is sick, and the whole heart faint","Only the hands were stained","The feet alone had wandered","The eyes only were dimmed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:5",verse:"Why should ye be stricken any more? ye will revolt more and more: the whole head is sick, and the whole heart faint.", insight:"Sin is pictured as a total condition, head to heart, not a few bad habits. This is why behavior repair alone never cures it, the whole person needs washing."},
{q:"What is said about the nation's wounds, bruises, and putrifying sores?", options:["They have not been closed, neither bound up, neither mollified with ointment","They were healed by the priests at once","They were hidden under fine linen","They were cured with the oil of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:6",verse:"From the sole of the foot even unto the head there is no soundness in it; but wounds, and bruises, and putrifying sores: they have not been closed, neither bound up, neither mollified with ointment.", insight:"Untreated wounds fester, and unconfessed sin does the same. The picture warns that ignoring sin never shrinks it, it only deepens the rot."},
{q:"Who was devouring Israel's land in the people's own presence?", options:["Strangers","The Assyrians by name","The Philistines alone","The men of the east"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:7",verse:"Your country is desolate, your cities are burned with fire: your land, strangers devour it in your presence, and it is desolate, as overthrown by strangers.", insight:"Judgment often arrives through ordinary losses, desolate fields and burned cities, before anyone names it as God's hand. The passage invites honest reading of trouble, not denial of it."},
{q:"The daughter of Zion was left like a cottage in a vineyard and a lodge in a garden of what?", options:["Cucumbers","Olives","Figs","Pomegranates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:8",verse:"And the daughter of Zion is left as a cottage in a vineyard, as a lodge in a garden of cucumbers, as a besieged city.", insight:"A lodge in a cucumber garden is a flimsy shelter left standing when harvest ends, the image of a city barely surviving. God paints the ruin plainly so His rescue will be seen clearly."},
{q:"Without a very small remnant, Israel would have become like which two cities?", options:["Sodom and Gomorrah","Tyre and Sidon","Babylon and Nineveh","Egypt and Edom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:9",verse:"Except the LORD of hosts had left unto us a very small remnant, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like unto Gomorrah.", insight:"God's judgment stops at a remnant because of His mercy, not because of Israel's merit. The verse teaches that survival itself is grace, never a proof of righteousness."},
{q:"What does the LORD say He is full of?", options:["The burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of fed beasts","The prayers of the priests alone","The songs of the temple choir","The gold of the eastern kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:11",verse:"To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me? saith the LORD: I am full of the burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of fed beasts; and I delight not in the blood of bullocks, or of lambs, or of he goats.", insight:"God was not hungry for sacrifices, He was sick of them, because they came from unwashed hands. Worship that is not joined to obedience is not devotion, it is noise."},
{q:"How does the LORD describe the incense of the disobedient?", options:["An abomination unto me","A sweet savor of rest","The perfume of heaven","A pleasing offering"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:13",verse:"Bring no more vain oblations; incense is an abomination unto me; the new moons and sabbaths, the calling of assemblies, I cannot away with; it is iniquity, even the solemn meeting.", insight:"What smells sweet to us can be an abomination to God when it covers rebellion. The heart behind the offering decides its worth, not the ceremony around it."},
{q:"What does the LORD say of the people's new moons and appointed feasts?", options:["My soul hateth them, they are a trouble unto me","They are my delight forever","They shall be remembered with joy","They are kept in heaven's record"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:14",verse:"Your new moons and your appointed feasts my soul hateth: they are a trouble unto me; I am weary to bear them.", insight:"God says He is weary of bearing their religion, strong language for a strong truth. Ritual that runs alongside injustice exhausts even infinite patience."},
{q:"Why will the LORD not hear the people's many prayers?", options:["Your hands are full of blood","Your voices are too low","Your words are too many","Your songs lack music"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:15",verse:"And when ye spread forth your hands, I will hide mine eyes from you: yea, when ye make many prayers, I will not hear: your hands are full of blood.", insight:"Blood on the hands blocks prayer, a sobering link between how we treat people and whether heaven hears us. Prayer is not a separate department of life, it rises out of all of it."},
{q:"What does the LORD command before He says, cease to do evil?", options:["Wash you, make you clean","Build me a higher altar","Bring me more offerings","Sing me a louder song"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:16",verse:"Wash you, make you clean; put away the evil of your doings from before mine eyes; cease to do evil;", insight:"Cleansing comes before conduct in this order. Repentance is not first doing better, it is first being washed, then the doing flows from the clean heart."},
{q:"Which of these does Isaiah say to do for the oppressed, the fatherless, and the widow?", options:["Relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow","Build them houses of cedar","Give them seats in the temple","Send them to the eastern lands"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:17",verse:"Learn to do well; seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow.", insight:"True religion is measured at the margins, with the oppressed, the orphan, and the widow. Learning to do well is learned by doing good to those who cannot repay it."},
{q:"Though your sins be as scarlet, what shall they become?", options:["White as snow","Clean as linen","Clear as water","Bright as silver"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:18",verse:"Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool.", insight:"Scarlet is the dye that never washes out, yet God promises the impossible stain will become snow. This is the gospel in a single verse, forgiveness that reaches the unfixable."},
{q:"What shall the willing and obedient eat?", options:["The good of the land","The fruit of the east","The bread of the priests","The grain of the valley"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:19",verse:"If ye be willing and obedient, ye shall eat the good of the land:", insight:"Willingness and obedience come before blessing in the order of the verse. God ties the good of the land to a responsive heart, so obedience is the door, not the reward."},
{q:"What will happen to those who refuse and rebel?", options:["They shall be devoured with the sword","They shall be sold to strangers","They shall be left without songs","They shall be carried to the east"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:20",verse:"But if ye refuse and rebel, ye shall be devoured with the sword: for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"The same mouth that promised blessing promises consequence, for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it. Both are certain, which makes the choice before the reader urgent and real."},
{q:"What do the rebellious princes love and follow after?", options:["Gifts and rewards","Honest weights","The poor's cause","The ancient laws"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:23",verse:"Thy princes are rebellious, and companions of thieves: every one loveth gifts, and followeth after rewards: they judge not the fatherless, neither doth the cause of the widow come unto them.", insight:"Leaders who love bribes stop judging the fatherless, because justice has a price and the poor cannot pay it. The verse warns that corruption at the top always lands hardest at the bottom."},
{q:"What will the LORD purely purge away?", options:["Thy dross","Thy gold","Thy walls","Thy vineyards"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 1:25",verse:"And I will turn my hand upon thee, and purely purge away thy dross, and take away all thy tin:", insight:"Purging dross is the smelter's refining fire, painful but purposeful. God's judgment on His own people is not destruction for its own sake, it is refinement toward righteousness."}
]},
{n:2, name:"The Mountain of the LORD", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 2:4",verse:"And he shall judge among the nations, and shall rebuke many people: and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more."}, facts:["Did you know the promise that nations will beat their swords into plowshares is engraved on a wall at the United Nations headquarters in New York?","Did you know Isaiah 2 was written over 2,700 years ago, yet it pictures a future where every nation streams to worship the one true God in Jerusalem?","Did you know the phrase walk in the light of the LORD in verse 5 became a favorite call to holy living among the prophets?"], questions:[
{q:"What was the word that Isaiah the son of Amoz saw concerning?", options:["Judah and Jerusalem","Samaria and Israel","Tyre and Sidon","Egypt and Cush"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:1",verse:"The word that Isaiah the son of Amoz saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem.", insight:"The vision is concerning Judah and Jerusalem, yet its reach runs to all nations. God starts with His own people and then widens the promise to the ends of the earth."},
{q:"In the last days, what shall be established in the top of the mountains?", options:["The mountain of the LORD's house","The walls of Jerusalem","The temple of the east","The towers of Zion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:2",verse:"And it shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’S house shall be established in the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills; and all nations shall flow unto it.", insight:"Mountains stand for kingdoms and powers, and God's house rises above them all. The picture teaches that no human power outlasts or outranks the reign of God."},
{q:"Who shall flow unto the exalted mountain of the LORD's house?", options:["All nations","The priests alone","The kings only","The tribes of Israel only"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:2",verse:"And it shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’S house shall be established in the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills; and all nations shall flow unto it.", insight:"All nations flowing upward to one mountain is the reversal of Babel's scattering. God's plan was never a tribal deity, it was always a world invitation."},
{q:"Why will the many people go up to the mountain of the LORD?", options:["He will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths","They will carry gold to the temple","They will hide from their enemies","They will trade in the courts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:3",verse:"And many people shall go and say, Come ye, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.", insight:"The nations come as students, not tourists, hungry to be taught God's ways. True worship begins with a teachable spirit, the willingness to learn before doing."},
{q:"From where shall go forth the law, and from where the word of the LORD?", options:["Out of Zion, and from Jerusalem","Out of Egypt, and from Tyre","Out of Babylon, and from Sidon","Out of the east, and from Tarshish"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:3",verse:"And many people shall go and say, Come ye, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.", insight:"God's word goes out from one small city to the whole world. The verse reminds us that great movements of truth often begin in unlikely, overlooked places."},
{q:"What will the nations beat their swords into?", options:["Plowshares","Chariot wheels","Temple pillars","City gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:4",verse:"And he shall judge among the nations, and shall rebuke many people: and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.", insight:"Swords become farming tools when the Judge of the nations has settled every dispute. Peace is the fruit of God's justice, not of human negotiation alone."},
{q:"What will the nations beat their spears into?", options:["Pruninghooks","Fishing nets","Ox goads","Shepherd's staffs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:4",verse:"And he shall judge among the nations, and shall rebuke many people: and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more.", insight:"Weapons of war become tools of harvest, a deliberate reversal. The instruments of death are remade for life, which is the shape of all God's redemption."},
{q:"What happens to the mean man and the great man?", options:["The mean man boweth down, and the great man humbleth himself","They are lifted up above the nations","They are hidden in the temple courts","They are clothed in white linen"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:9",verse:"And the mean man boweth down, and the great man humbleth himself: therefore forgive them not.", insight:"High and low alike bow down, no rank escapes the humbling. God levels every human hierarchy before He alone is exalted."},
{q:"What does Isaiah urge the house of Jacob to walk in?", options:["The light of the LORD","The shadow of the hills","The paths of the east","The ways of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:5",verse:"O house of Jacob, come ye, and let us walk in the light of the LORD.", insight:"Before the nations walk up the mountain, Jacob is told to walk in the light now. Those closest to the promise carry the first responsibility to live it."},
{q:"The house of Jacob had been replenished from what direction with foreign ways?", options:["The east","The west","The north","The south"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:6",verse:"Therefore thou hast forsaken thy people the house of Jacob, because they be replenished from the east, and are soothsayers like the Philistines, and they please themselves in the children of strangers.", insight:"God's people were filling up on eastern practices, a quiet drift before open idolatry. Compromise usually begins with borrowing customs, not with denying God outright."},
{q:"What was the people's land full of?", options:["Silver and gold, horses and chariots","Wheat and barley, oil and wine","Sheep and cattle, tents and camels","Timber and stone, iron and bronze"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:7",verse:"Their land also is full of silver and gold, neither is there any end of their treasures; their land is also full of horses, neither is there any end of their chariots:", insight:"Wealth and weapons had multiplied beyond measure, yet the nation was spiritually bankrupt. The verse warns that full treasuries and full stables can coexist with an empty heart."},
{q:"What did the people worship?", options:["The work of their own hands, that which their own fingers have made","The stars of the morning sky","The rivers of the deep earth","The winds of the desert"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:8",verse:"Their land also is full of idols; they worship the work of their own hands, that which their own fingers have made:", insight:"They bowed to what their own fingers made, worshipping their own skill reflected back at them. Idolatry is often self worship wearing a costume."},
{q:"In that day, who alone shall be exalted?", options:["The LORD alone","The kings of Judah","The priests of the temple","The prophets of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:11",verse:"The lofty looks of man shall be humbled, and the haughtiness of men shall be bowed down, and the LORD alone shall be exalted in that day.", insight:"Every human height is brought low so that the LORD alone stands exalted. The day of the LORD ends all rival claims to glory, including our own."},
{q:"Which tall trees does the day of the LORD come upon?", options:["The cedars of Lebanon and the oaks of Bashan","The palms of Jericho and the sycamores of the plain","The olives of Gethsemane and the figs of Bethany","The pines of Hermon and the myrtles of Sharon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:13",verse:"And upon all the cedars of Lebanon, that are high and lifted up, and upon all the oaks of Bashan,", insight:"The tallest trees picture the proudest powers, and even Lebanon's cedars are not too high to fall. Nothing rooted in pride stands in the day of the LORD."},
{q:"What man made things will the day of the LORD come upon?", options:["Every high tower, and every fenced wall","Every market stall and merchant ship","Every vineyard terrace and winepress","Every sheepfold and threshing floor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:15",verse:"And upon every high tower, and upon every fenced wall,", insight:"Towers and walls are the pride of self defense, the belief that we can secure ourselves. God comes against the very things we trusted instead of Him."},
{q:"Upon which ships will the day of the LORD come?", options:["The ships of Tarshish","The ships of Tyre","The ships of Egypt","The ships of Joppa"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:16",verse:"And upon all the ships of Tarshish, and upon all pleasant pictures.", insight:"The ships of Tarshish carried the wealth of distant trade, the pride of commerce. God reaches across the sea after the riches men counted on."},
{q:"What will the LORD utterly do to the idols in that day?", options:["Abolish them","Polish them","Sell them","Hide them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:18",verse:"And the idols he shall utterly abolish.", insight:"Idols will not be reformed or repurposed, they will be utterly abolished. God does not negotiate with false gods, He removes them."},
{q:"To whom will a man cast his idols of silver and gold in that day?", options:["To the moles and to the bats","To the priests of the temple","To the merchants of Tyre","To the poor of the land"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:20",verse:"In that day a man shall cast his idols of silver, and his idols of gold, which they made each one for himself to worship, to the moles and to the bats;", insight:"Idols once polished and displayed end up thrown to creatures of the dark, which is where they always belonged. The verse shows the shameful end of everything trusted instead of God."},
{q:"Why will people go into the holes of the rocks and caves of the earth?", options:["For fear of the LORD, and for the glory of his majesty","To hide their treasures from thieves","To escape the armies of the east","To find water in the desert"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:19",verse:"And they shall go into the holes of the rocks, and into the caves of the earth, for fear of the LORD, and for the glory of his majesty, when he ariseth to shake terribly the earth.", insight:"When He ariseth to shake terribly the earth, hiding places become the only plan. Better to bow willingly now than to cower in the rocks then."},
{q:"Why should we cease from man, according to Isaiah's closing word?", options:["His breath is in his nostrils, wherein is he to be accounted of","His heart is full of deceit","His days are few and evil","His wisdom is as chaff"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 2:22",verse:"Cease ye from man, whose breath is in his nostrils: for wherein is he to be accounted of?", insight:"A man's whole life hangs on the breath in his nostrils, a fragile thread. Trusting man over God is leaning on something that could stop breathing at any moment."}
]},
{n:3, name:"Judgment on Jerusalem's Leaders", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 3:10",verse:"Say ye to the righteous, that it shall be well with him: for they shall eat the fruit of their doings."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 3 contains one of the longest lists of clothing and jewelry in the Bible, more than twenty items of finery stripped from the proud daughters of Zion?","Did you know this chapter pictures society turned upside down, with children as princes and the young insulting the old, as a sign of God's judgment?","Did you know the line the spoil of the poor is in your houses is one of the Bible's sharpest charges that corrupt leaders steal from the vulnerable?"], questions:[
{q:"What two stays will the LORD take away from Jerusalem and Judah?", options:["The whole stay of bread, and the whole stay of water","The whole stay of gold, and the whole stay of silver","The whole stay of cedar, and the whole stay of stone","The whole stay of horses, and the whole stay of chariots"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:1",verse:"For, behold, the Lord, the LORD of hosts, doth take away from Jerusalem and from Judah the stay and the staff, the whole stay of bread, and the whole stay of water,", insight:"Bread and water are the basics of life, and God removes the very foundations when a nation despises Him. Judgment often begins with the ordinary things we took for granted."},
{q:"Which of these will the LORD take away from Jerusalem?", options:["The judge","The shepherd","The tax collector","The merchant"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:2",verse:"The mighty man, and the man of war, the judge, and the prophet, and the prudent, and the ancient,", insight:"When God removes the judge, the prophet, and the prudent, He is removing the nation's wisdom keepers. A people that will not listen eventually loses the voices it ignored."},
{q:"Which skilled worker is named among those the LORD will take away?", options:["The cunning artificer","The potter of the valley","The weaver of the hills","The farmer of the plain"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:3",verse:"The captain of fifty, and the honourable man, and the counsellor, and the cunning artificer, and the eloquent orator.", insight:"Even the cunning artificer, the master craftsman, is listed among the losses. When judgment comes, it touches every trade and talent, not only the religious offices."},
{q:"Who will be given as princes over the people?", options:["Children, and babes shall rule over them","Strangers from the east","Elders from the gate","Priests from the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:4",verse:"And I will give children to be their princes, and babes shall rule over them.", insight:"Children as rulers is not a cute image here, it is a curse, leadership without wisdom or weight. A nation that will not be governed by God ends up governed by immaturity."},
{q:"How will the child behave toward the ancient?", options:["Proudly","Humbly","Silently","Tenderly"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:5",verse:"And the people shall be oppressed, every one by another, and every one by his neighbour: the child shall behave himself proudly against the ancient, and the base against the honourable.", insight:"When the young despise the old, the order of honor has collapsed. Respect for elders is one of the first casualties of a society coming apart."},
{q:"A man will seize his brother and say, Thou hast what, be thou our ruler?", options:["Clothing","Silver","Horses","Vineyards"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:6",verse:"When a man shall take hold of his brother of the house of his father, saying, Thou hast clothing, be thou our ruler, and let this ruin be under thy hand:", insight:"Leadership has sunk so low that owning a coat qualifies a man to rule. Desperation makes people grab at anyone, which is why character in leaders matters before the crisis comes."},
{q:"Why does the seized man refuse to be a ruler?", options:["In my house is neither bread nor clothing","My sons are too young for war","My fields lie in the east","My name is not written in the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:7",verse:"In that day shall he swear, saying, I will not be an healer; for in my house is neither bread nor clothing: make me not a ruler of the people.", insight:"He swears he will not be a healer because he has nothing to give, an honest admission in a dishonest age. Better an honest refusal than a proud man taking a job he cannot do."},
{q:"What had provoked the eyes of the LORD's glory?", options:["Their tongue and their doings","Their songs and their dances","Their feasts and their markets","Their walls and their towers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:8",verse:"For Jerusalem is ruined, and Judah is fallen: because their tongue and their doings are against the LORD, to provoke the eyes of his glory.", insight:"Tongue and doings together, words and works, provoked God's glory. What we say and what we do are both on record before Him."},
{q:"The people declared their sin as openly as which city?", options:["Sodom","Gomorrah","Babel","Nineveh"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:9",verse:"The shew of their countenance doth witness against them; and they declare their sin as Sodom, they hide it not. Woe unto their soul! for they have rewarded evil unto themselves.", insight:"They hide it not, sin paraded without shame. When evil stops blushing, judgment is near, because the conscience has already fallen silent."},
{q:"What shall the righteous eat?", options:["The fruit of their doings","The bread of the temple","The grain of the valley","The honey of the hills"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:10",verse:"Say ye to the righteous, that it shall be well with him: for they shall eat the fruit of their doings.", insight:"The righteous eat the fruit of their own doings, a harvest, not a wage. God lets every life ripen into what it truly was, which is both warning and comfort."},
{q:"What shall be given to the wicked?", options:["The reward of his hands","The praise of the gate","The seat of honor","The portion of kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:11",verse:"Woe unto the wicked! it shall be ill with him: for the reward of his hands shall be given him.", insight:"The wicked receive the reward of their own hands, exactly what they earned. Justice means no one is cheated, which comforts the victim and warns the oppressor."},
{q:"Who are called the people's oppressors?", options:["Children, and women rule over them","Strangers from the north","Priests from the east","Merchants from the sea"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:12",verse:"As for my people, children are their oppressors, and women rule over them. O my people, they which lead thee cause thee to err, and destroy the way of thy paths.", insight:"Leaders who cause God's people to err and destroy the way of their paths are named as a judgment. Misleading the people is itself one of the heaviest sins a leader can commit."},
{q:"What have the leaders eaten up?", options:["The vineyard","The harvest of wheat","The flocks of the field","The storehouses of oil"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:14",verse:"The LORD will enter into judgment with the ancients of his people, and the princes thereof: for ye have eaten up the vineyard; the spoil of the poor is in your houses.", insight:"The vineyard belonged to the people, yet the rulers devoured it. Leadership that consumes what it was meant to protect has inverted its calling."},
{q:"What does the LORD accuse the leaders of doing to the poor?", options:["Grinding their faces and beating them to pieces","Driving them from the city","Taxing their vineyards twice","Selling them to strangers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:15",verse:"What mean ye that ye beat my people to pieces, and grind the faces of the poor? saith the LORD GOD of hosts.", insight:"Grinding the faces of the poor is violent, deliberate cruelty by the powerful. God hears the cry of the crushed, and He interrogates their crushers."},
{q:"How did the daughters of Zion walk?", options:["With stretched forth necks and wanton eyes","With bowed heads and covered faces","With hurried steps and downcast eyes","With bare feet and ashes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:16",verse:"Moreover the LORD saith, Because the daughters of Zion are haughty, and walk with stretched forth necks and wanton eyes, walking and mincing as they go, and making a tinkling with their feet:", insight:"Stretched necks and wanton eyes are the posture of pride on display. Haughtiness is not only felt inside, it walks, and God sees the walk."},
{q:"What will the LORD do to the crown of their heads?", options:["Smite it with a scab","Crown it with thorns","Cover it with ashes","Shave it with a razor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:17",verse:"Therefore the LORD will smite with a scab the crown of the head of the daughters of Zion, and the LORD will discover their secret parts.", insight:"The crown of the head, the place of their adornment, becomes the place of their shame. God answers pride at the very point where it showed itself."},
{q:"Which ornaments will the LORD take away from the daughters of Zion?", options:["Their tinkling ornaments about their feet","Their veils of fine linen","Their sandals of leather","Their baskets of fruit"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:18",verse:"In that day the Lord will take away the bravery of their tinkling ornaments about their feet, and their cauls, and their round tires like the moon,", insight:"The tinkling anklets that drew attention will be silenced. What was worn to be noticed will be removed, because pride's decorations do not survive God's day."},
{q:"Which jewelry is listed among the finery taken away?", options:["The rings, and nose jewels","The crowns of gold","The scepters of ivory","The seals of bronze"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:21",verse:"The rings, and nose jewels,", insight:"Even small treasures are catalogued, ring by ring, showing how thoroughly God searches. Nothing we prize is too small to be noticed, or too precious to lose."},
{q:"Instead of sweet smell, what shall there be?", options:["Stink","Smoke","Dust","Ashes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:24",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that instead of sweet smell there shall be stink; and instead of a girdle a rent; and instead of well set hair baldness; and instead of a stomacher a girding of sackcloth; and burning instead of beauty.", insight:"Sweet smell turns to stink, beauty to burning, every adornment inverted. Pride's perfume cannot cover decay, and God will not let it pretend."},
{q:"Where shall desolate Zion sit?", options:["Upon the ground","Upon the mountains","By the rivers","Within the gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 3:26",verse:"And her gates shall lament and mourn; and she being desolate shall sit upon the ground.", insight:"Her gates lament and she sits upon the ground, the posture of mourning. When all props are gone, the ground is where humility finally begins."}
]},
{n:4, name:"A Branch of Beauty After Judgment", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 4:2",verse:"In that day shall the branch of the LORD be beautiful and glorious, and the fruit of the earth shall be excellent and comely for them that are escaped of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 4 is one of the shortest chapters in the book, only six verses, and it turns like a hinge from judgment in chapter 3 to hope for the remnant?"], questions:[
{q:"How many women shall take hold of one man in that day?", options:["Seven","Three","Five","Ten"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:1",verse:"And in that day seven women shall take hold of one man, saying, We will eat our own bread, and wear our own apparel: only let us be called by thy name, to take away our reproach.", insight:"Seven women to one man shows how few men the wars will leave, a stark picture of loss. The verse measures judgment by the empty places it leaves behind."},
{q:"What shall be upon all the glory?", options:["A defence","A crown of gold","A song of praise","A wall of fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:5",verse:"And the LORD will create upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, and upon her assemblies, a cloud and smoke by day, and the shining of a flaming fire by night: for upon all the glory shall be a defence.", insight:"Upon all the glory shall be a defence, God's presence guarding what He has restored. What God glorifies, He also protects."},
{q:"Why do the women want to be called by the man's name?", options:["To take away their reproach","To share his inheritance","To enter the city gates","To escape the famine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:1",verse:"And in that day seven women shall take hold of one man, saying, We will eat our own bread, and wear our own apparel: only let us be called by thy name, to take away our reproach.", insight:"Reproach, the shame of being alone and unprotected, is what they beg to lose. Being named by another pictures the deeper human hunger to belong somewhere safe."},
{q:"What shall the branch of the LORD be in that day?", options:["Beautiful and glorious","Tall and terrible","Hidden and silent","Small and despised"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:2",verse:"In that day shall the branch of the LORD be beautiful and glorious, and the fruit of the earth shall be excellent and comely for them that are escaped of Israel.", insight:"After the stripping of chapter 3, the branch of the LORD appears beautiful and glorious. God grows hope out of the very ground judgment had laid bare."},
{q:"For whom shall the fruit of the earth be excellent and comely?", options:["Them that are escaped of Israel","The kings of the east","The merchants of Tyre","The priests of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:2",verse:"In that day shall the branch of the LORD be beautiful and glorious, and the fruit of the earth shall be excellent and comely for them that are escaped of Israel.", insight:"The escaped ones, the remnant, inherit the excellent fruit. God's promises narrow to the faithful few and then widen again through them."},
{q:"What shall he that is left in Zion be called?", options:["Holy","Great","Mighty","Wise"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:3",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that he that is left in Zion, and he that remaineth in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, even every one that is written among the living in Jerusalem:", insight:"Those left are called holy, not merely spared. Survival through judgment is meant to produce consecration, not just relief."},
{q:"Who shall be called holy in Jerusalem?", options:["Every one that is written among the living","Every man of war","Every elder of the gate","Every firstborn son"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:3",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that he that is left in Zion, and he that remaineth in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, even every one that is written among the living in Jerusalem:", insight:"Being written among the living is God's own registry, not man's. The verse teaches quiet assurance, that God keeps the names of His own."},
{q:"By what two spirits will the LORD purge the blood of Jerusalem?", options:["The spirit of judgment, and the spirit of burning","The spirit of wisdom, and the spirit of counsel","The spirit of might, and the spirit of knowledge","The spirit of grace, and the spirit of peace"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:4",verse:"When the Lord shall have washed away the filth of the daughters of Zion, and shall have purged the blood of Jerusalem from the midst thereof by the spirit of judgment, and by the spirit of burning.", insight:"Washing comes by judgment and burning, not by ignoring the filth. God cleanses thoroughly, which is why His purification, though painful, is never superficial."},
{q:"What will the LORD create over Mount Zion by day and by night?", options:["A cloud and smoke by day, and a flaming fire by night","A wall of stone by day, and a watch of angels by night","A river of water by day, and a canopy of stars by night","A sound of trumpets by day, and a song of praise by night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:5",verse:"And the LORD will create upon every dwelling place of mount Zion, and upon her assemblies, a cloud and smoke by day, and the shining of a flaming fire by night: for upon all the glory shall be a defence.", insight:"The cloud and fire recall the wilderness journey, God dwelling over His people as He did at Sinai. After judgment, He returns to camp among them, which is the true restoration."},
{q:"What will the tabernacle be for the people?", options:["A shadow from the heat, a refuge, and a covert from storm and rain","A storehouse for grain and oil","A court for judging the nations","A school for teaching the law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 4:6",verse:"And there shall be a tabernacle for a shadow in the daytime from the heat, and for a place of refuge, and for a covert from storm and from rain.", insight:"God's presence becomes shelter, shade in the heat and cover in the storm. The end of the promise is not only forgiveness but safety, a place to hide in God Himself."}
]},
{n:5, name:"The Vineyard Song and Six Woes", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 5:20",verse:"Woe unto them that call evil good, and good evil; that put darkness for light, and light for darkness; that put bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter!"}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 5 is built around a love song about a vineyard, which Jesus later echoed in His parable of the wicked tenants?","Did you know this chapter contains six solemn woes, pronounced against greed, drunkenness, mockery, moral confusion, pride, and corrupt judges?","Did you know the Hebrew word for the vineyard's wild grapes suggests stinking, rotten fruit, showing how far the harvest fell from the owner's hope?"], questions:[
{q:"What song does Isaiah sing to his wellbeloved?", options:["A song of his beloved touching his vineyard","A song of war against the nations","A song of mourning for the fallen","A song of praise in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:1",verse:"Now will I sing to my wellbeloved a song of my beloved touching his vineyard. My wellbeloved hath a vineyard in a very fruitful hill:", insight:"The song is sung to the wellbeloved, yet it ends as an accusation against the vineyard. Love that has done everything right has the standing to ask why the fruit failed."},
{q:"Where did the wellbeloved have his vineyard?", options:["In a very fruitful hill","In the valley of the east","By the waters of the sea","On the plains of the north"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:1",verse:"Now will I sing to my wellbeloved a song of my beloved touching his vineyard. My wellbeloved hath a vineyard in a very fruitful hill:", insight:"The hill was very fruitful, the location was perfect, so the failure cannot be blamed on the ground. The problem was never God's provision, it was the vine's response."},
{q:"What did the beloved build in the midst of the vineyard?", options:["A tower","A wall of cedar","A gate of bronze","A house of stone"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:2",verse:"And he fenced it, and gathered out the stones thereof, and planted it with the choicest vine, and built a tower in the midst of it, and also made a winepress therein: and he looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.", insight:"A tower means the owner invested in watching over and protecting his vines. God does not plant and abandon, He tends, which makes unfaithfulness all the more serious."},
{q:"What did the vineyard bring forth instead of grapes?", options:["Wild grapes","Thorns and thistles","Bitter olives","Sour figs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:2",verse:"And he fenced it, and gathered out the stones thereof, and planted it with the choicest vine, and built a tower in the midst of it, and also made a winepress therein: and he looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.", insight:"Wild grapes look like the real thing from a distance but are worthless up close. The verse warns against a faith that has the shape of fruit without its substance."},
{q:"What question does the LORD ask about His vineyard?", options:["What could have been done more to my vineyard, that I have not done in it","Who planted thorns among my vines","Where have my watchmen gone","Why have the rains failed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:4",verse:"What could have been done more to my vineyard, that I have not done in it? wherefore, when I looked that it should bring forth grapes, brought it forth wild grapes?", insight:"God asks what more He could have done, and the silence is the answer. When every grace has been given, the fault lies with the receiver, not the giver."},
{q:"What will the LORD do first to His vineyard?", options:["Take away the hedge thereof","Burn the tower within it","Sell the winepress","Uproot the choicest vine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:5",verse:"And now go to; I will tell you what I will do to my vineyard: I will take away the hedge thereof, and it shall be eaten up; and break down the wall thereof, and it shall be trodden down:", insight:"Removing the hedge withdraws protection, the first and gentlest judgment. God often begins discipline by simply stepping back and letting consequences in."},
{q:"What will come up in the wasted vineyard?", options:["Briers and thorns","Cedars and oaks","Wheat and barley","Olives and figs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:6",verse:"And I will lay it waste: it shall not be pruned, nor digged; but there shall come up briers and thorns: I will also command the clouds that they rain no rain upon it.", insight:"An unpruned vineyard does not stay empty, it fills with briers. Neglected hearts never stay neutral, something always grows, and without tending it will be thorns."},
{q:"What will the LORD command the clouds concerning the vineyard?", options:["That they rain no rain upon it","That they pour out hail upon it","That they gather over the tower","That they hide the sun from it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:6",verse:"And I will lay it waste: it shall not be pruned, nor digged; but there shall come up briers and thorns: I will also command the clouds that they rain no rain upon it.", insight:"Withholding rain is withdrawing blessing itself. The verse shows that God's favor, like rain, is a gift we cannot manufacture once He stays His hand."},
{q:"What is the vineyard of the LORD of hosts?", options:["The house of Israel","The city of Tyre","The land of Egypt","The mountains of the east"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:7",verse:"For the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel, and the men of Judah his pleasant plant: and he looked for judgment, but behold oppression; for righteousness, but behold a cry.", insight:"The riddle is solved, the vineyard is Israel, and every hearer knew it meant them. God's parables always land personally in the end."},
{q:"The LORD looked for judgment, but behold what?", options:["Oppression","Silence","Darkness","Famine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:7",verse:"For the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel, and the men of Judah his pleasant plant: and he looked for judgment, but behold oppression; for righteousness, but behold a cry.", insight:"He looked for judgment and found oppression, a deliberate wordplay in Hebrew. The crop came up as the opposite of the seed, which is the tragedy of the whole chapter."},
{q:"Because they cast away the law of the LORD, what shall their root be as?", options:["Rottenness","A stone","Chaff","Dust"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:24",verse:"Therefore as the fire devoureth the stubble, and the flame consumeth the chaff, so their root shall be as rottenness, and their blossom shall go up as dust: because they have cast away the law of the LORD of hosts, and despised the word of the Holy One of Israel.", insight:"A rotten root cannot hold up a blossom, so their blossom goes up as dust. Despising God's word decays a people from the inside before the collapse shows outside."},
{q:"Woe unto them that join what to what?", options:["House to house, and field to field","Silver to gold, and gem to gem","Ship to ship, and sail to sail","Tower to tower, and wall to wall"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:8",verse:"Woe unto them that join house to house, that lay field to field, till there be no place, that they may be placed alone in the midst of the earth!", insight:"Joining house to house pictures greed swallowing every neighbor's land until none are left. Wealth that leaves no room for others is not success, it is theft by another name."},
{q:"What will become of the many great and fair houses?", options:["Desolate, without inhabitant","Filled with strangers from the east","Sold for silver and gold","Given to the poor of the land"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:9",verse:"In mine ears said the LORD of hosts, Of a truth many houses shall be desolate, even great and fair, without inhabitant.", insight:"The mansions built by greed will stand empty, desolate without inhabitant. What is gained by crowding others out ends as lonely as the sin that built it."},
{q:"How much shall ten acres of vineyard yield?", options:["One bath","Ten baths","One ephah","Ten ephahs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:10",verse:"Yea, ten acres of vineyard shall yield one bath, and the seed of an homer shall yield an ephah.", insight:"Ten acres yielding one bath is a harvest of almost nothing, futility written in numbers. Greed's fields produce meager returns because God frustrates unjust gain."},
{q:"Woe unto them that rise up early in the morning for what purpose?", options:["To follow strong drink","To gather manna","To watch the sunrise","To tend the vineyard"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:11",verse:"Woe unto them that rise up early in the morning, that they may follow strong drink; that continue until night, till wine inflame them!", insight:"They rise early not for work but for wine, devotion to pleasure from dawn to night. What we give our first hours to reveals our true master."},
{q:"What do the feasters not regard?", options:["The work of the LORD, neither the operation of his hands","The songs of the temple","The counsel of the elders","The needs of the poor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:12",verse:"And the harp, and the viol, the tabret, and pipe, and wine, are in their feasts: but they regard not the work of the LORD, neither consider the operation of his hands.", insight:"Music and wine filled their feasts, but God's works went unnoticed. Pleasure that never looks up at the Giver becomes its own kind of blindness."},
{q:"What hath enlarged herself and opened her mouth without measure?", options:["Hell","The sea","The desert","The pit of famine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:14",verse:"Therefore hell hath enlarged herself, and opened her mouth without measure: and their glory, and their multitude, and their pomp, and he that rejoiceth, shall descend into it.", insight:"Hell enlarging herself is a dreadful image of judgment making room for the proud. The verse teaches that rejection of God does not end in nothingness, it ends somewhere."},
{q:"Woe unto them that call evil good and good evil. What do they put for light?", options:["Darkness","Shadow","Night","Gloom"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:20",verse:"Woe unto them that call evil good, and good evil; that put darkness for light, and light for darkness; that put bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter!", insight:"Swapping the names of good and evil does not change their nature, it only blinds the speaker. Moral confusion is itself a judgment, and this woe names it plainly."},
{q:"Woe unto them that are wise in what?", options:["In their own eyes","In the eyes of kings","In the sight of the priests","In the judgment of elders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:21",verse:"Woe unto them that are wise in their own eyes, and prudent in their own sight!", insight:"Wisdom in one's own eyes needs no teacher and takes no correction. Self satisfied cleverness is the one wisdom that guarantees folly."},
{q:"Whom do the corrupt justify for reward?", options:["The wicked","The stranger","The poor","The fatherless"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 5:23",verse:"Which justify the wicked for reward, and take away the righteousness of the righteous from him!", insight:"Justifying the wicked for reward is justice sold to the highest bidder. When verdicts can be bought, the righteous lose twice, first to the crime, then to the court."}
]}
]);
